# FluidEQ build-time configuration.
#
# Copy this file to `.env` and fill in the values you want. `.env` is
# gitignored; this example is the only one that gets committed.
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# Variables already set in the shell or in CI win over this file, so a release
# pipeline can inject its own values without editing anything.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# EVERYTHING IN THIS FILE IS PUBLIC.
#
# webpack inlines these into the renderer bundle, so every value ends up
# readable in the shipped JavaScript. Only put things here that are public by
# nature. A Stripe *Payment Link* and a receiving Bitcoin address qualify —
# they exist to be handed out. A Stripe SECRET KEY (sk_live_..., sk_test_...)
# or any other credential absolutely does not: putting one here ships it to
# every user. FluidEQ never needs one, because it only ever opens a URL.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------


# --- Optional: contribution destinations ------------------------------------
#
# Leave any of these blank to hide that option. Leave *every* destination in
# this file blank — Stripe, Buy Me a Coffee and all of the crypto addresses —
# and the "Support the work" entry disappears from the app entirely. A build
# with no destination shows no donate button rather than a broken one.

# Stripe Payment Link, e.g. https://buy.stripe.com/xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx
# Create one at https://dashboard.stripe.com/payment-links — a Payment Link
# needs no server and no API key, which is why it is the right fit here.
# Must be https: a plaintext URL is rejected so payment routing cannot be
# downgraded in transit.
FLUIDEQ_STRIPE_URL=

# Buy Me a Coffee or Ko-fi page, e.g. https://buymeacoffee.com/yourname
# Also https-only.
FLUIDEQ_COFFEE_URL=


# --- Optional: crypto addresses ---------------------------------------------
#
# Every one of these is independent — fill in only the chains you actually
# hold, and the rest stay hidden. Each is shape-checked before it is shown, so
# a placeholder or a pasted URL is ignored rather than displayed as a
# destination.
#
# VERIFY EVERY VALUE YOU PASTE. These are checked for *shape*, not for
# checksum: a typo that still looks like an address will be displayed, and
# funds sent to it are unrecoverable. The app also shows the network next to
# each address, because several of these formats are shared across chains.

# bech32 `bc1...`, or legacy `1...` / `3...`
FLUIDEQ_BITCOIN_ADDRESS=

# `0x...` — WARNING: every EVM chain uses this format. The address shown is
# labelled Ethereum mainnet, so do not put a Polygon/BSC-only address here.
FLUIDEQ_ETHEREUM_ADDRESS=

# `ltc1...`, or legacy `L...` / `M...`
FLUIDEQ_LITECOIN_ADDRESS=

# `D...`
FLUIDEQ_DOGECOIN_ADDRESS=

# `4...` or `8...` (95 characters)
FLUIDEQ_MONERO_ADDRESS=

# base58, 32-44 characters
FLUIDEQ_SOLANA_ADDRESS=

# Shelley-era `addr1...`
FLUIDEQ_CARDANO_ADDRESS=

# `T...` — TRC-20 network
FLUIDEQ_TRON_ADDRESS=

# Label a wallet shows when opening a payment URI.
# Defaults to "FluidEQ development".
FLUIDEQ_CRYPTO_LABEL=

# Where people can contribute time instead of money.
# Defaults to https://github.com/StartSWest/FluidEQ
FLUIDEQ_REPOSITORY_URL=


# --- Optional: where a share post sends people ------------------------------
#
# The share card carries this URL, and it is read by people who have never
# seen FluidEQ — so it should hand them an installer, not a source tree.
# Defaults to the releases page of FLUIDEQ_REPOSITORY_URL, which is right for
# a GitHub project; set it only if downloads live somewhere else.
#
# FLUIDEQ_DOWNLOAD_URL=https://fluideq.app/download


# --- Optional: the account backend -------------------------------------------
#
# Leave these blank and FluidEQ has no accounts at all: no sign-in, no
# community, no leaderboard, and every visualizer that ships in this source
# still works exactly as it does today. That is the right default, and it is
# what a fork should keep unless it stands up a backend of its own.
#
# Both or none. A build with one of them shows a sign-in form that cannot
# complete, which is worse than no form, so anything short of the pair turns
# the feature off.
#
# The project URL must be https and a bare origin: no path, no query, no
# fragment. A path is rejected rather than trimmed, because silently dropping
# half of what somebody typed produces a build that talks to the wrong endpoint.

# The Supabase project origin, e.g. https://abcdefghijklm.supabase.co
FLUIDEQ_SUPABASE_URL=

# The project's PUBLISHABLE key — `sb_publishable_...`, from Supabase's API
# settings. This one is designed to be handed to every client; row-level
# security in the database is what protects data, not the secrecy of this key.
#
# NOTHING ELSE IS ACCEPTED. Not `sb_secret_...`, and not the legacy JWT keys
# that begin `eyJ...` either — an anon JWT and a service-role JWT are
# indistinguishable from the outside, and a service-role key pasted here would
# ship to every user and bypass every database policy. A build with the wrong
# shape gets no accounts rather than a leaked master credential. If your project
# still issues legacy keys, rotate to publishable ones; Supabase recommends it
# regardless.
FLUIDEQ_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=

# Optional: the monthly price of FluidEQ Plus, the amount alone, as text, e.g.
# "$5". The app adds "/ month" in the reader's own language; a period written
# here would stay English in all ten. This is
# also the switch for the offer — blank means signing in works but no upgrade
# is shown, which is every build until a merchant account exists. Text rather
# than a number, because the checkout page shows the real figure with the
# buyer's own tax, and anything the app computed would disagree with it
# somewhere in the world. The checkout itself is minted by the server for the
# signed-in account when the button is pressed; nothing about it is configured
# here, and the app never sees a card.
FLUIDEQ_PLUS_PRICE=

# Optional: the yearly price of FluidEQ Plus, the amount alone, e.g. "$50".
# Offered beside the monthly one with "/ year" in the reader's language, and in
# the Plus terms. Blank means monthly only. Ignored without a monthly price.
# Must match the yearly option on the merchant's membership page.
FLUIDEQ_PLUS_PRICE_YEARLY=

# Optional: where the server-side functions answer (checkout, billing portal).
# Defaults to the Supabase project's own gateway, <FLUIDEQ_SUPABASE_URL>/functions/v1,
# which is right until they move under the product's domain — then this is the
# one line that changes, e.g. https://fluideq.com/api. https only; a path is
# fine, a query or fragment is not, and a trailing slash is dropped.
# FLUIDEQ_API_URL=


# --- Development only: look at the premium path before a backend exists -------
#
# Runtime variables for the main process, honoured only when the app is not
# packaged — a release build has no way to set them. Neither loosens anything:
# a pack still has to verify against the key compiled into the app, and the
# server still serves packs only to a paying account. In development the main
# process reads this file at startup (`dev-main.cjs` loads it for the public
# values and the rest come along; a value already in the shell wins), so the
# simplest place for them is your own gitignored `.env`. The shell works too:
#
#   $env:FLUIDEQ_DEV_ENTITLED = '1'
#   $env:FLUIDEQ_DEV_SCENE_PACKS_DIR = 'D:\DEV\_PERSONAL\fluideq-premium\dist'
#   $env:FLUIDEQ_DEV_COMMUNITY_SAMPLE = '1'
#   pnpm dev
#
# FLUIDEQ_DEV_ENTITLED=1 pretends the signed-in account has Plus, in the app
# alone, with no server involved — for a checkout with no backend at all.
# FLUIDEQ_DEV_SCENE_PACKS_DIR points at the signing tool's output; every
# *.envelope.json there is adopted at startup as if the server had sent it.
# Nothing to set for the pretend membership: in every development build the
# Plus card in the Account panel carries a button that asks the server to send
# the merchant's own signed membership event for this account — a payment, or
# a cancellation — so everything after the card charge can be watched without
# a card. The merchant has no test mode; this is the next best thing, the
# server cannot tell the difference, and it answers admins only.
# FLUIDEQ_DEV_COMMUNITY_SAMPLE=1 lays a cast of twelve sample people, their
# messages and their listening hours over the Community tab and the
# leaderboard, so both can be looked at full before anyone is in them. Nothing
# is written to the server; the real messages and the real board stay as they
# are underneath.


# --- Optional: the private route on the bug report dialog --------------------
#
# One address, shown to nobody but written into every build made from this
# checkout. Public by nature: it ends up readable in the shipped JavaScript,
# like everything else in this file.
#
# Left empty the dialog offers only the GitHub issue, which is the right
# default for a fork — a build that is not yours should not send its bugs to
# somebody who cannot fix them.
#
# FLUIDEQ_SUPPORT_EMAIL=support@example.com
